"""Flashcard format (hint + the math + airplane translation + breakdown): CMO topics 14-19."""
B = {
1401: (r"What does the pilot do when a gravity step would carry him into the mountain?",
  [r"<strong>$x_k-\alpha_k\nabla f(x_k)$ = the plain downhill step</strong> (nose along $-\nabla f$, flight distance $\alpha_k$).",
   r"<strong>$\Pi_\Omega$ = air-traffic control:</strong> if the step lands outside the airspace, it puts you on the nearest legal point.",
   r"<strong>Equivalent form:</strong> $\arg\min_{y\in\Omega}\{\nabla f^\top(y-x_k)+\frac1{2\alpha_k}\|y-x_k\|^2\}$ = the best legal point under a linear slope plus a leash of strength $\frac1{\alpha_k}$."]),
1402: (r"What does a stationary parking spot look like to the projection operator?",
  [r"<strong>$x^*-\alpha\nabla f(x^*)$:</strong> gravity shoves the plane into the wall.",
   r"<strong>$\Pi_\Omega(\cdot)$ puts it straight back on $x^*$,</strong> so $G_\alpha(x^*)=0$: no net motion.",
   r"<strong>Equivalent:</strong> $-\nabla f(x^*)$ lies in the normal cone, i.e. points straight into the wall, so no legal direction goes downhill."]),
1403: (r"How fast does the pilot close in on the best parking spot?",
  [r"<strong>Convex, smooth terrain:</strong> $\frac{L\|x_0-x^*\|^2}{2k}$, the gap shrinks like $1/k$ (sublinear).",
   r"<strong>Strongly convex terrain:</strong> $c\rho^k$ with $\rho\lt1$, a fixed fraction of the distance removed each leg (linear).",
   r"<strong>The walls do not change either rate:</strong> projection is non-expansive, so it never undoes progress."]),
1404: (r"How does the pilot test a leg when the step slides along the wall?",
  [r"<strong>$x(\alpha)=\Pi_\Omega(x_k-\alpha\nabla f(x_k))$ traces an arc along the wall</strong> as $\alpha$ changes.",
   r"<strong>$\frac\gamma\alpha\|x_k-x(\alpha)\|^2$ is the promised drop:</strong> big for a long actual displacement, scaled by the leash $\frac1\alpha$.",
   r"<strong>Try $\alpha=s,\beta s,\beta^2s,\dots$ and keep the first that earns it.</strong>"]),
1405: (r"How does the pilot get back onto a curved runway after a straight step?",
  [r"<strong>$y=x_k-\alpha P_k\nabla f(x_k)$:</strong> project gravity onto the tangent plane so you slide along the wall (nose $P_k\nabla f$).",
   r"<strong>The straight step leaves the curved surface,</strong> so $h(y)\ne0$ measures how far off you are.",
   r"<strong>$-\nabla h[\nabla h^\top\nabla h]^{-1}h(y)$ = drop along the normal</strong> by exactly the amount needed to restore $h=0$."]),
1406: (r"Which coordinate gets clamped and where does the plane end up?",
  [r"<strong>$x-\alpha\nabla f=(1,1)-(-1,5)=(2,-4)$:</strong> the step dives below the ground ($x_2\lt0$).",
   r"<strong>$\max(0,\cdot)$ clamps the negative coordinate:</strong> the plane lands on the ground at $(2,0)$.",
   r"<strong>The second step gives $(2,0)$ again:</strong> a fixed point, so the method has found the stationary point."]),
1501: (r"What equation does the pilot solve while sliding along the walls he is touching?",
  [r"<strong>$A_{\mathcal W}p=0$:</strong> the move $p$ must keep every wall in the working set fixed (slide along the rails).",
   r"<strong>$Qp+g=A_{\mathcal W}^\top\lambda$:</strong> inside that rail, balance the quadratic's slope against the wall pushes.",
   r"<strong>The KKT system solves both at once,</strong> giving the step $p_k$ and the pushes $\lambda_k$."]),
1502: (r"How far can the pilot roll before another wall stops him?",
  [r"<strong>$\frac{b_i-a_i^\top x_k}{a_i^\top p_k}$ = distance to wall $i$:</strong> the gap divided by how fast you approach it.",
   r"<strong>Only walls with $a_i^\top p_k\lt0$ matter</strong> (you are heading toward them); the minimum over them is the first wall you hit.",
   r"<strong>$\min(1,\cdot)$:</strong> a full step $\alpha=1$ if nothing blocks, otherwise stop at the wall and add it to $\mathcal W$."]),
1503: (r"When does the pilot release a wall, and which one?",
  [r"<strong>$p_k=0$:</strong> sliding along the current walls goes nowhere, you are at the lowest point of that runway.",
   r"<strong>$\lambda_j\lt0$:</strong> that wall would have to pull, which walls cannot do: gravity actually wants you off it.",
   r"<strong>Drop the most negative $\lambda_j$</strong> and the next step leaves that wall and gains altitude."]),
1504: (r"Why does the flight always end in finitely many legs?",
  [r"<strong>$Q\succ0$ and $\alpha_k\gt0$:</strong> every real leg strictly lowers the altitude.",
   r"<strong>So no working set ever repeats,</strong> because a repeat would mean the same altitude twice.",
   r"<strong>There are only $2^m$ possible working sets,</strong> hence at most $2^m$ legs."]),
1505: (r"What happens when several walls meet at the plane's position?",
  [r"<strong>$b_i-a_i^\top x_k=0$ for a wall outside the working set:</strong> it is already touching, so the blocking distance is 0.",
   r"<strong>$\alpha_k=0$:</strong> the plane does not move; only the working set changes.",
   r"<strong>The altitude stays equal,</strong> so cycling is possible: an anti-cycling rule fixes the order walls enter and leave."]),
1506: (r"Why does the plane end at $(2,2)$ with both pushes positive?",
  [r"<strong>Start at $(0,0)$ with walls $x_1=0,x_2=0$;</strong> the pushes say drop $x_1=0$ first.",
   r"<strong>Slide along $x_2=0$ to the blocking wall $x_1=2$,</strong> reaching $(2,0)$; then drop $x_2=0$.",
   r"<strong>At $(2,2)$ both pushes are $\lambda=2\gt0$,</strong> so no wall wants to be dropped: the optimum."]),
1601: (r"How does the ruler under the altitude curve give the dual value?",
  [r"<strong>$\omega(z)$ = lowest legal altitude when the wall is moved by $z$.</strong>",
   r"<strong>$\omega(z)+\mu^\top z$ = that curve plus a tilted ruler of slope $\mu$;</strong> the lowest point of the sum is $\phi(\mu)$, where the ruler touches the curve from below.",
   r"<strong>$\phi(\mu)=\inf_x\{f+\mu^\top g\}$:</strong> the same quantity, computed directly from the Lagrangian."]),
1602: (r"Why can the best floor never pass the true lowest altitude?",
  [r"<strong>$q(\lambda,\mu)$ = the floor built from prices $(\lambda,\mu)$.</strong>",
   r"<strong>For any legal $x$ and $\mu\ge0$,</strong> the Lagrangian $f(x)+\mu^\top g(x)\le f(x)$ because $g\le0$, so the floor is below every legal altitude.",
   r"<strong>Hence $d^*\le p^*$ for every problem:</strong> the gap $p^*-d^*\ge0$ is the duality gap."]),
1603: (r"What two conditions make the floor touch the lowest altitude exactly?",
  [r"<strong>Convexity:</strong> the primal function $\omega$ is convex, so a supporting ruler exists.",
   r"<strong>Slater's room to move:</strong> a strictly interior point guarantees the ruler is not vertical.",
   r"<strong>Then $p^*=d^*$:</strong> the pilot from above and the tower from below meet."]),
1604: (r"Why is the dual function always concave, however wild the terrain?",
  [r"<strong>For a fixed plan $x$, $f(x)+\mu^\top g(x)$ is a straight line in $\mu$.</strong>",
   r"<strong>$q$ is the lowest of all those lines</strong> (a pointwise infimum).",
   r"<strong>A pointwise minimum of straight lines bends only one way:</strong> $q(\alpha y_1+(1-\alpha)y_2)\ge\alpha q(y_1)+(1-\alpha)q(y_2)$."]),
1605: (r"What does a multiplier tell the pilot about moving a wall?",
  [r"<strong>$p^*(z)$ = lowest legal altitude after moving the wall by $z$.</strong>",
   r"<strong>$p^*(z)\ge p^*(0)-\mu^{*\top}z$:</strong> a wall moved back by $z$ cannot save more than $\mu^*z$.",
   r"<strong>So $\nabla_zp^*(0)=-\mu^*$:</strong> the multiplier is the price of one unit of extra airspace."]),
1606: (r"What quantity does the margin strip depend on, and why do we minimise $\|w\|$?",
  [r"<strong>$y_i(w^\top x_i+b)\ge1$:</strong> every plane is on its correct side and at least a margin away.",
   r"<strong>Margin $=2/\|w\|$:</strong> the strip width shrinks as $w$ grows.",
   r"<strong>Maximising the margin = minimising $\tfrac12\|w\|^2$,</strong> a convex quadratic problem."]),
1608: (r"Which planes carry weight in the dual, and why does the kernel trick work?",
  [r"<strong>$w^*=\sum\alpha_i^*y_ix_i$:</strong> the strip's normal is a weighted sum of planes.",
   r"<strong>$\alpha_i^*\gt0$ only for planes on the strip's edge (support vectors);</strong> all others have weight 0.",
   r"<strong>The dual uses data only through $x_i^\top x_j$,</strong> so replacing it by a kernel changes the geometry without changing the algorithm."]),
1701: (r"Why does restricting to $\nabla_x\mathcal L=0$ give a usable dual problem?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla_x\mathcal L=0$ = wall pushes balance gravity:</strong> the plane is at equilibrium.",
   r"<strong>For a convex valley, equilibrium is the lowest point of $\mathcal L(\cdot,\mu)$,</strong> so no inner minimisation is needed.",
   r"<strong>Maximising $\mathcal L$ over $(x,\lambda,\mu)$ subject to equilibrium</strong> gives a smooth problem with the same value."]),
1702: (r"What do the cockpit and the tower report at the optimum?",
  [r"<strong>$f,g_j$ convex and $h_i$ affine:</strong> a convex valley.",
   r"<strong>The Wolfe dual value $\mathcal L(x,\lambda,\mu)$ at the best equilibrium</strong> equals the primal lowest altitude.",
   r"<strong>Zero gap:</strong> the maximum on one side equals the minimum on the other."]),
1703: (r"What are the cockpit and the tower each optimising for a quadratic program?",
  [r"<strong>Primal:</strong> minimise $\tfrac12x^\top Qx+c^\top x$ subject to $Ax\ge b$.",
   r"<strong>Dual objective $-\tfrac12x^\top Qx+b^\top y$:</strong> the same quadratic with the opposite sign plus the resource value.",
   r"<strong>Constraint $Qx-A^\top y+c\ge0$:</strong> the equilibrium condition; at the optimum the two objectives are equal."]),
1704: (r"Why can the equilibrium trick fail on a hill?",
  [r"<strong>$f=-x^2$ has $f'(x)=0$ only at 0,</strong> which is its maximum, so the 'equilibrium' value is $f(0)=0$.",
   r"<strong>True minimum $=-\infty$:</strong> the dual value 0 is above the primal value, $d^*\gt p^*$.",
   r"<strong>Without convexity,</strong> force balance can happen at a peak or saddle."]),
1705: (r"How does the tower decide whether to raise or lower the price of a wall?",
  [r"<strong>$x(\mu_k)=\arg\min\mathcal L(x,\mu_k)$:</strong> the plane flies to its best spot under the current prices.",
   r"<strong>$g(x(\mu_k))$ = violation:</strong> positive means the plane is flying into the wall, so $\mu$ goes up.",
   r"<strong>$\max(0,\cdot)$ keeps prices non-negative:</strong> $\alpha_kg$ is the subgradient step of the dual."]),
1706: (r"What shape does the entropy-maximising traffic take?",
  [r"<strong>$q(\lambda)=b^\top\lambda-\sum_ie^{a_i^\top\lambda-1}$:</strong> the dual is smooth, unconstrained and concave, easy to maximise.",
   r"<strong>The optimal primal is $x_i\propto e^{a_i^\top\lambda-1}$:</strong> exponential, the Boltzmann form.",
   r"<strong>$\lambda$ plays the role of an inverse temperature</strong> set by the energy budget."]),
1801: (r"Who is minimising what, and who is maximising what?",
  [r"<strong>Primal (airline):</strong> $\min c^\top x$ with $Ax=b$, $x\ge0$: the cheapest schedule meeting demand.",
   r"<strong>Dual (authority):</strong> $\max b^\top y$ with $A^\top y\le c$: highest revenue without pricing any route above its cost.",
   r"<strong>Weak duality:</strong> revenue never exceeds cost; at the optimum they are equal."]),
1807: (r"Why are corners the only places the plane can be pinned?",
  [r"<strong>$x_N=0$:</strong> the non-basic variables are zero, i.e. those walls are touched.",
   r"<strong>$x_B=B^{-1}b$:</strong> the remaining variables are determined by the independent walls through the corner (needs $B$ invertible).",
   r"<strong>$x_B\ge0$ makes the corner legal,</strong> so basic feasible solutions are exactly the vertices."]),
1802: (r"Why must the lowest point of a tilted sheet over a polygon be a corner?",
  [r"<strong>The altitude is linear,</strong> so there are no hills or valleys inside the polygon.",
   r"<strong>Moving along any direction raises or lowers the altitude at a constant rate,</strong> so you can keep going until you hit a wall, then slide, until a corner.",
   r"<strong>So an optimum can always be found among the finitely many basic feasible solutions.</strong>"]),
1803: (r"Which pairs of statements must hold together at the optimum?",
  [r"<strong>$x_j(c_j-A_j^\top y)=0$:</strong> a route is flown only if its fee equals its cost.",
   r"<strong>$y_i(A_ix-b_i)=0$:</strong> a resource has a positive price only if it is fully used.",
   r"<strong>Either factor can be zero,</strong> but never both strictly positive: the same on/off switch as KKT."]),
1804: (r"What does an infeasible primal force the dual to do?",
  [r"<strong>$A^\top y\ge0,\ b^\top y\lt0$ (Farkas):</strong> the walls cannot hold gravity.",
   r"<strong>$d=-y$ then has $A^\top d\le0$ and $b^\top d\gt0$:</strong> a direction that keeps the dual feasible and increases its objective.",
   r"<strong>$b^\top(y_0+td)\to\infty$:</strong> the dual is unbounded."]),
1805: (r"How does the pilot know when to stop hopping between corners?",
  [r"<strong>$x_B=B^{-1}b\ge0,\ x_N=0$:</strong> the current corner.",
   r"<strong>Reduced cost $c_N^\top-c_B^\top B^{-1}N$:</strong> how the objective changes per unit of moving along each edge.",
   r"<strong>If all reduced costs are $\ge0$, no neighbouring corner is lower,</strong> so this corner is optimal."]),
1808: (r"What keeps the interior-point plane away from the walls?",
  [r"<strong>$-\mu\sum\ln x_j$ is the barrier:</strong> it blows up as any $x_j\to0$, repelling the plane from the walls.",
   r"<strong>$c^\top x$ still pulls toward lower cost.</strong>",
   r"<strong>The minimiser $x(\mu)$ traces the central path;</strong> as $\mu\to0$ the barrier fades and the plane reaches the optimal corner."]),
1806: (r"Where do the cockpit's and the tower's numbers meet?",
  [r"<strong>Primal:</strong> $3(2)+2(3)=12$ at $(2,3)$. <strong>Dual:</strong> $5(1)+7(1)=12$ at $(1,1)$.",
   r"<strong>Every feasible primal value is at most 12 and every feasible dual value at least 12;</strong> they meet exactly at 12.",
   r"<strong>Complementary slackness explains why:</strong> positive $x_j$ forces the matching dual constraint to be tight."]),
1901: (r"How big is the fine for a given amount of violation?",
  [r"<strong>$\max(0,g_j)^2$:</strong> no fine inside the airspace, a quadratic fine outside; squaring keeps it smooth at the wall.",
   r"<strong>$\tfrac{c_k}2h_i^2$:</strong> equality walls are fined for any deviation in either direction.",
   r"<strong>As $c_k\to\infty$ the fine becomes so steep</strong> that the unconstrained minimiser is forced onto the boundary."]),
1902: (r"What stops the plane from ever touching the wall?",
  [r"<strong>$-\ln(-g_j)$ is finite inside</strong> ($g_j\lt0$) and $\to+\infty$ as $g_j\to0^-$.",
   r"<strong>Adding $\frac1cB(x)$ to $f$ keeps the plane strictly inside;</strong> as $c$ grows the barrier weakens and the plane approaches the boundary from inside.",
   r"<strong>Needs a strict interior:</strong> that is Slater's condition again."]),
1903: (r"Why does a huge fine make the pilot zig-zag?",
  [r"<strong>Across the wall the curvature grows like $c$;</strong> along the runway it stays $O(1)$.",
   r"<strong>$\kappa=\lambda_{\max}/\lambda_{\min}\approx O(c)\to\infty$:</strong> the valley becomes a razor-thin canyon.",
   r"<strong>Gradient and Newton steps then lose accuracy:</strong> catastrophic ill-conditioning."]),
1904: (r"How do you read the wall's push from the penalty iterates?",
  [r"<strong>$h_i(x(c_k))$ = how far the plane sinks into the forbidden zone.</strong>",
   r"<strong>Multiplying by the stiffness $c_k$ gives the restoring force,</strong> like a spring: $\lambda_i^{(k)}=c_kh_i$.",
   r"<strong>As $c_k\to\infty$ the depth $\to0$ but the product converges to the true multiplier $\lambda^*_i$.</strong>"]),
1905: (r"What does the tower adjust so the fine does not have to grow forever?",
  [r"<strong>$h(x_k)$ = remaining violation after the current leg.</strong>",
   r"<strong>$c_kh(x_k)$ = the correction the multiplier needs:</strong> the same force-from-depth reading as penalty recovery.",
   r"<strong>$\lambda_{k+1}=\lambda_k+c_kh(x_k)$</strong> converges without sending $c_k\to\infty$, so the problem stays well conditioned."]),
1906: (r"How does $\lambda$ climb to its target in the worked sequence?",
  [r"<strong>$\lambda_0=0$ gives $x^{(0)}=(2/3,2/3)$,</strong> a plane still short of the boundary.",
   r"<strong>$\lambda_1=-4/3$ gives $x^{(1)}=(8/9,8/9)$, and $\lambda_2=-16/9$ is next:</strong> each update closes a fixed fraction of the remaining gap.",
   r"<strong>The multiplier converges to $\lambda^*$ and the plane lands on the constraint</strong> with the penalty $c$ held fixed."]),
}
FIX = {cid: {"hint": h, "breakdown": b} for cid, (h, b) in B.items()}
